Stronger isolation; supports parallel test execution (each test owns its DB). Adds ~50 ms per test.

let session;
beforeEach(async () => {
session = client.startSession();
session.startTransaction();
});
afterEach(async () => {
await session.abortTransaction();
await session.endSession();
});
test('insert + query', async () => {
await db.collection('users').insertOne({ name: 'Ada' }, { session });
// ... assertions using { session } on every op
});
Fast (no actual writes), but every test must thread { session } everywhere — easy to forget. Tests can't exercise code paths that themselves use transactions.

Aggregation pipelines are easy to break subtly. Snapshot the output to lock down the contract:
test('daily revenue rollup', async () => {
await seedOrders();
const result = await db.collection('orders').aggregate([
{ $match: { status: 'paid' } },
{ $group: { _id: { $dateTrunc: { date: '$at', unit: 'day' } }, revenue: { $sum: '$total' } } },
{ $sort: { _id: 1 } },
]).toArray();
expect(result).toMatchSnapshot();
});
Jest / Vitest store the result; future runs compare. On intentional change, update snapshot.
For Python: syrupy or pytest-snapshot.

